\markup {
  \pad-around #1
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      In May 1747 Bach visited Frederick the Great at his court in Potsdam. The King, a keen flautist,
      played him a theme of his own and asked him to improvise a fugue on it, which Bach did at once in
      three parts on one of the royal Silbermann fortepianos. Asked for a fugue in six parts, he declined
      to improvise one on so demanding a subject. Back in Leipzig he worked the royal theme out in a
      series of pieces, printed the same year and dedicated to the King as the \italic { Musikalisches Opfer }
      (Musical Offering): two ricercars, ten canons and a trio sonata for flute, violin and continuo. The
      title of the first ricercar forms the acrostic \italic { Regis Iussu Cantio Et Reliqua Canonica Arte
      Resoluta } (“At the King's command, the song and the remainder resolved with canonic art”). The
      Ricercar a 3 is thought to stand close to the Potsdam improvisation, while the Ricercar a 6, printed
      in open score with each voice on its own staff, is Bach's answer to the King's challenge and one of
      the summits of his contrapuntal art. Both are presented here arranged for piano.
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #1
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      The \italic { thema regium } is a long, severe subject in C minor: it rises through the tonic triad,
      falls by a diminished seventh and then descends chromatically back to the tonic, a line rich in
      harmonic possibilities but awkward to combine with itself. Unlike the canons and the sonata, the
      two ricercars were plainly conceived for the keyboard. The Ricercar a 3 was printed on two staves,
      and Bach's autograph of the Ricercar a 6, which survives in Berlin, is likewise written as a
      keyboard score; the open score of the printed edition was chosen to make the six-part
      counterpoint easier to read rather than to call for six instruments.
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #1
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      The term \italic { ricercar } (from the Italian \concat { \italic ricercare , } “to seek out”) first
      described free, prelude-like pieces for lute or keyboard in early sixteenth-century Italy. It
      later came to mean a strictly imitative composition in which a theme is worked out through all
      the voices, and in this sense the ricercars of Frescobaldi and Froberger are direct ancestors of
      the fugue. By 1747 the word was already old-fashioned, and Bach's choice of it, also echoed in the
      acrostic, points to the learned, archaic style of these two pieces.
  }
}

\markup {
  \pad-around #1
  \wordwrap \abs-fontsize #10 {
      The story of the Potsdam visit was reported in the Berlin press a few days later and retold by
      Wilhelm Friedemann Bach, who accompanied his father, to Johann Nikolaus Forkel for his 1802
      biography of the composer. The \italic { Musical Offering } has since fascinated performers,
      composers and writers alike: Anton Webern orchestrated the Ricercar a 6 in 1935, splitting the
      royal theme into a mosaic of instrumental colours, and Douglas Hofstadter took the work's canons
      and riddles as a recurring motif of \italic { Gödel, Escher, Bach } (1979).
  }
}
